course: dictionary
lesson: 2844
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Dock is a good example. Read the words around it, then ask: what would fit here?
explain: «A robot vacuum returns to its dock to charge.» Here a dock is a stand that holds a device and charges it. A harbour does not fit this sentence, so that meaning is wrong here.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If you put «harbour» in place of dock in the vacuum sentence, it stops making sense. That tells you the right meaning is the charging stand.
explain: Read claims carefully too. «Rubbing with dock leaves may help a little, but there is little proof» does not say they cure a sting. Notice the words may and little.
fact: Dock can be a noun (a place, a stand, a weed) or a verb (to arrive and join up, or to take part of something away).
fact: A dock is a place in a harbour where ships stop to load or unload.
fact: A dock can be a stand that holds and charges a device, like a robot vacuum's dock.
fact: To dock a spacecraft means it arrives and joins up, for example with a space station.
fact: To dock marks or pay means to take part of them away.
exercise: «A robot vacuum returns to its dock to charge.» What is a dock in this sentence? | A type of weed | *A stand that holds and charges a device | A place where ships stop | A place in court where the accused stands
exercise: In the rhyme «wall and fall, or dock and clock», which word rhymes with dock? | fall | wall | *clock | nettle
exercise: «Rubbing with dock leaves may help a little, but there is little proof.» What does this sentence tell you? | Dock leaves make nettle stings worse | Dock leaves cure every nettle sting for ever | *The leaves may help a little, but this is not proven | Dock leaves are poisonous
test: «The ship will dock at the harbour at six o'clock.» What does dock mean in this sentence? | *To arrive and stop at a harbour | A stand that charges a device | To take part of something away | A weed with big leaves
test: The teacher says, «The school will dock five marks for late homework.» What does dock mean here? | A place in court where the accused stands | A place where ships load | To arrive and join up | *To take part of something away
exam: «The accused stood in the dock while the judge spoke.» What is the dock here? | A charging stand for a device | A weed with big leaves | A harbour where ships stop | *The place in court where the accused person stands
